          zfswatcher - ZFS pool monitoring and notification daemon

                           version history


version 0.7.0 (unreleased)

Upgrade notes

 - zfswatcher now saves the pool state to "statefile" (default
   /var/lib/zfswatcher/state.json, set "statefile =" to disable) and
   reports after a restart only the changes since the last run instead of
   treating every pool and device as new. The bundled systemd unit creates
   the directory (StateDirectory=); with a custom unit or init script
   the directory must be writable for the service user. The state file is
   written atomically with mode 0640. Add the "statefile" line to your
   configuration if you want to change the location.

New features

 - State persistence: zfswatcher remembers the pool state across restarts
   (see upgrade notes) and reports only the changes since the last run.
 - The web interface has a health check at "<rootdir>/healthz" for monitoring
   tools such as Uptime Kuma. It needs no login and reveals nothing about
   pools: 200 "ok" if the last "zpool status" query worked and is not older
   than three times "zpoolstatusrefresh", otherwise 503 with "starting",
   "stale" or "error".
 - New option "commandtimeout" in section [main]: the time in seconds
   (1 to 3600, default 60) after which a hanging zpool/zfs command is
   killed. It is read at startup only.
 - New option "trustedproxies" in the [www] section: IP addresses or
   networks of reverse proxies whose X-Forwarded-For and
   X-Forwarded-Proto headers are trusted. The default (empty) ignores the
   headers. Failed web logins are now logged as warnings with the client
   address. The README has nginx and Caddy examples; the proxy must pass
   the original Host header on ("$http_host" in nginx, CSRF protection).

Fixes

 - The web server now shuts down gracefully on SIGTERM/SIGINT: running
   requests get up to 5 seconds to finish and no new ones are accepted.
 - A pool whose configuration cannot be determined is now shown without
   devices instead of with a bogus device named "The".
 - The iostat stream reader no longer loses entries that arrive together
   in a single read.
 - A failed or malformed iostat read no longer crashes the daemon.

Cleanup

 - Removed etc/gccgo-build.sh, which built from the bundled golibs/ tree.
 - The project homepage and the RPM spec now point to the maintained fork
   at github.com/tantefred/zfswatcher. The about page names the fork.
 - The RPM spec documents the Go 1.26 requirement but does not check it as a
   build dependency, so the package builds with a Go toolchain from elsewhere.


version 0.6.0 released 2026-11-22

Upgrade notes

 - Web passwords: MD5 crypt hashes ("$1$...") keep working but are
   deprecated and logged at startup; create a bcrypt hash with
   "zfswatcher -P" and put it into your configuration. If a web user still
   has the example password "toor", zfswatcher warns at startup and the web
   pages show a red notice.
 - "zpoolstatusrefresh" and "zfslistrefresh" must be between 1 and 86400
   seconds; other values are now rejected as configuration errors.
 - Configurations from 0.4.x with the old 9 column zfslistcmd and
   zfslistusagecmd work again, but updating them to the commands of the
   example configuration is recommended (zfswatcher logs a warning).
 - Switching LEDs on or off ("locate") in the web interface needs POST and
   rejects cross-origin requests; a script calling it with GET must be
   changed. State changing requests are checked against the Origin header,
   so a reverse proxy has to pass the Host header (and Origin) unchanged.
 - New log files are created with mode 0640; existing files keep their mode.

Security

 - Web interface hardening: the HTTP server has timeouts and a header size
   limit, all responses carry security headers (Content-Security-Policy,
   X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, and HSTS with
   TLS). Switching an LED on or off ("locate") now requires POST, rejects
   cross-origin requests (CSRF), accepts only known devices and no longer
   redirects to the Referer header.

 - Web passwords are now stored as bcrypt hashes ("$2a$..."). "zfswatcher -P"
   reads the password without echo (twice on a terminal, one line from
   stdin otherwise) and prints a bcrypt hash; the example configuration uses
   a bcrypt hash of "toor". The unmaintained github.com/abbot/go-http-auth
   is no longer used: HTTP Basic authentication is built in, and unknown or
   disabled users take as much time to reject as wrong passwords.

 - zfswatcher logs a warning at startup and the web pages show a red notice
   if an enabled web user still has the example password "toor".

 - Bundled web assets updated: Bootstrap 3.3.7 -> 3.4.1 (fixes several XSS
   issues, CVE-2018-14040, CVE-2018-14042, CVE-2019-8331) and jQuery
   3.2.1 -> 3.7.1. The bundled Bootstrap license file is now the MIT
   license text. The dead html5shim script (loaded over http, for IE 8 and
   older) is removed from the web pages.

 - E-mail notifications use the standard library net/smtp instead of the
   unmaintained github.com/snabb/smtp. STARTTLS is used when the server
   offers it, connections time out after 30 seconds, and the messages now
   carry Message-ID, MIME-Version and UTF-8 headers (non-ASCII subjects are
   encoded). The configuration is unchanged.

 - New log files are created with mode 0640 instead of 0666.

Fixes

 - Configurations from 0.4.x with the 9 column zfslistcmd/zfslistusagecmd
   (without logicalused and compressratio) work again; since 0.5.0 all
   usage data and the used space warnings were missing for them. The
   dashboard shows "-" for the unknown columns and zfswatcher logs a warning
   once, recommending to update the commands to the example configuration.

 - Errors are no longer ignored silently. A pid file which cannot be written
   (for example an old "pidfile = /var/run/zfswatcher.pid" while running as
   the user zfswatcher) is now logged as an error; the daemon keeps running.
   A failing "locate" request in the web interface is logged and answered
   with an error. "zpoolstatusrefresh" and "zfslistrefresh" must be between
   1 and 86400 seconds; other values are rejected as configuration errors.

Packaging and CI

 - New man page zfswatcher-ledctl(8). The Debian postinst no longer runs
   "chown -R" on /var/log/zfswatcher; systemd sets the ownership via
   LogsDirectory= when the service starts.

 - The FreeBSD and Solaris builds are now vetted in CI; FreeBSD without cgo
   compiles with stubs (the real FreeBSD binary still needs cgo).

version 0.5.0 released 2026-11-21

 - First release of the tantefred fork. Linux (Debian, Proxmox VE) first.

 - Builds with Go modules; the bundled golibs/ directory is gone.

 - Understands current OpenZFS (2.x) output: "0B" sizes, remove:, checkpoint:,
   expand: and condense: sections, scrub and resilver reported together,
   special, dedup, log, cache and spare classes, dRAID, and the file list of
   "zpool status -v".

 - Fixed wrong arguments in the "device state changed" notifications.

 - The Debian package runs the daemon as the unprivileged system user
   "zfswatcher" with a systemd unit (the SysV init script is gone). The
   unit creates /var/log/zfswatcher and hardens the service. The bundled
   zfswatcher-ledctl and its udev rule are installed, so enclosure LEDs work
   without root. If you use ledctl from the ledmon package, add User=root
   with "systemctl edit zfswatcher". Logrotate now rotates
   /var/log/zfswatcher/*.log.

 - The web interface now listens on 127.0.0.1:8081 by default instead of
   all interfaces on port 80, for use behind a reverse proxy. Change "bind"
   in the [www] section to listen elsewhere, for example ":8081" for all
   interfaces. Ports below 1024 need CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE for the service
   user (see the comment in the systemd unit).

 - The Linux binary is static and built without cgo. It no longer crashes
   with "Illegal instruction" on virtual CPUs without AVX.

 - External commands (zpool, zfs) are killed after 60 seconds, so a hanging
   "zpool status" no longer stops the monitoring.

 - Pools in state SUSPENDED now raise an alert. If you maintain your own
   /etc/zfs/zfswatcher.conf, add SUSPENDED:alert to "poolstatemap" and
   SUSPENDED:text-error to "poolstatecssclassmap"; states missing from these
   maps are treated as severity info.

 - Test suite with real Proxmox VE and Debian 13 fixtures, CI with lint,
   formatting, race detector and vulnerability checks.


version 0.3 released 2013-02-20

 - Disk space usage warnings at configurable thresholds.

 - Experimental FreeBSD and OpenIndiana support.

 - Reconfigure on SIGHUP.

 - Ubuntu ZFS PPA dependency fix.

 - Other minor improvements.


version 0.2 released 2013-02-04

 - Compatibility fix for changes in ZoL 0.6.0-rc14 output formatting.

 - Other minor improvements.


version 0.01 released 2013-02-03

 - Initial release.

